\A{pgpkeys} Verifying KiTTY++ downloads

\I{verifying new versions}KiTTY++ releases carry no \i{GPG signatures}.
They carry \i{Authenticode} signatures, \c{SHA256SUMS} files and
programs that check themselves. Download KiTTY++ only from
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}{\c{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/releases}}.

\H{pgpkeys-signed} What is signed

Every \c{.exe} (in the installers, the ZIPs and on the ISO image) and
both \c{.msi} installers are signed, with a trusted timestamp, by:

\c KAPPER NETWORK-COMMUNICATIONS GmbH

ZIP and ISO files have no signature of their own: check the files
inside. The unsigned files of a package (help file,
\c{kitty.ini.example}, \c{README-BETA.md}) are covered by
\c{SHA256SUMS}.

\H{pgpkeys-authenticode} Checking the signature

Explorer: \q{Properties}, \q{Digital Signatures}, select the entry,
\q{Details}: \q{This digital signature is OK.} No such tab means no
signature. PowerShell, for an \c{.exe} or \c{.msi}:

\c Get-AuthenticodeSignature kitty.exe |
\c   Format-List Status, SignerCertificate

\c{Status} must be \c{Valid}, the subject must begin with
\c{CN=KAPPER NETWORK-COMMUNICATIONS GmbH}. The built-in update check
deletes a downloaded installer unrun if it fails this test.

\H{pgpkeys-sha256sums} Checking the SHA256SUMS file

Each ZIP and each ISO folder contains \c{SHA256SUMS}, one SHA-256
hash per file (the 32-bit package also \c{SHA1SUMS}). Installers are
not listed; their signature is their check.

\c certutil -hashfile kitty.exe SHA256
\c Get-FileHash kitty.exe -Algorithm SHA256

Compare with the file's line; case does not matter. \c{README-BETA.md} has a
one-line command that checks all files and prints \c{OK} or \c{FAIL}
for each. Windows XP has no SHA-256 tool: check the 32-bit package on
a newer Windows first.

\H{pgpkeys-selfcheck} The programs check themselves

A release build checks at every start:

\b Its file name must begin with its own or the matching PuTTY name,
ignoring case (\c{kitty (1).exe} runs), or it stops with
\q{\cw{Renamed executable}}.

\b Its Authenticode signature: it refuses when the file was changed,
its signature removed or re-signed by somebody else (\q{\cw{Signature check
failed}}), never because Windows cannot judge the signature (Windows
XP, offline Windows 7). Nothing is fetched from the network.

\b Its release stamp, an Ed25519 signature over the file's SHA-256
by a key made for that release only, needing no Windows cryptography:
\q{\cw{Integrity check failed}} with \c{modified}, \c{truncated},
\c{bad stamp} or \c{no stamp}, also in the Application event log
(source \c{KiTTY++}). A hidden program that cannot read its own file
refuses with \c{cannot check}; a visible one starts.

\c{kitty_pterm.exe} and \c{kitty_tel.exe} are stamped but do not check
themselves; builds from source have none of this. \q{Application >
Security > Applications} in the configuration box shows the state of
each KiTTY++ program file beside the running one (red = changed,
foreign-signed, unchecked or other version). The checks make tampering
harder; they are not a security boundary.

\H{pgpkeys-fail} When a check fails

Do not run the file; download it again. If a file from the release
page fails, report it privately (\q{Report a vulnerability} at
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/security}{\c{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/security}})
with file name, version and the exact message, not as a public issue.

\H{pgpkeys-pgpfp} The \c{-pgpfp} option

The \i\c{\-pgpfp} option, inherited from PuTTY, prints the
\i{PGP key fingerprint}s of the PuTTY master keys. They prove nothing
about KiTTY++ files. The option is deprecated.
